            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue!\r\n\r\nTag textobject has obvious limitations in 'mini.ai' due to it using Lua pattern matching by default. The current implementation [does recognize only alphanumeric characters in tag name](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/f158f1905286e023d5848ab0b335a1cf5b8c692a/lua/mini/ai.lua#L1082). You can modify in `setup()` call as [it is suggested in this comment](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/314#issuecomment-1529097699) (it will recognize alphanumeric and punctuation as tag name):\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nrequire('mini.ai').setup({\r\n  custom_textobjects = {\r\n    t = { '<([%p%w]-)%f[^<%w][^<>]->.-</%1>', '^<.->().*()</[^/]->$' }\r\n  }\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nHowever, specifically for tags a more sustainable and robust approach would be to use tree-sitter parsing and textobjects based on queries from 'nvim-treesitter-textobject': create 'after/ftplugin/html.lua' file and define `vim.b.miniai_config` buffer local variable there:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal spec_treesitter = require('mini.ai').gen_spec.treesitter\r\nvim.b.miniai_config = {\r\n  custom_textobjects = {\r\n    t = spec_treesitter({ a = '@function.outer', i = '@function.inner' }),\r\n  },\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt will be more accurate but won't work inside comments and strings, though.",
